# .paraspace/Parafile, per-project config for para. Sourced as bash, it sets the
# few PARA_* variables para itself reads (version, image, what to clone, routes).
# Everything else is a hook (.paraspace/hooks/). A real env var overrides any of these.
#
# Active below are only the keys a project actually has to decide. Everything
# para defaults sensibly is listed commented-out at the bottom, so you can see
# what's available without this file carrying a stale copy of para's defaults.
#
# The full schema is docs/parafile.md in the package.

# para contract version these hooks target. para refuses (clearly) on a mismatch.
: "${PARA_CONTRACT:=1}"

# Project identity, the key para uses for ownership, `para ls` scoping, and the
# shared-volume name (para-home-<PARA_PROJECT>). It DEFAULTS to this directory's
# name, which is usually what you want, so it is left unset here. Declare it to
# pin an identity that survives renaming the checkout:
# : "${PARA_PROJECT:=my-project}"

# What `para image build` builds FROM: the incus image its builder launches, and
# one `sh -c` line run inside it before .paraspace/hooks/image-build. NEITHER has
# a default, because para never picks your distro, and a para update must not be
# able to change it under you. Any incus image works (`images:debian/13`,
# `images:alpine/edge`, …); this template targets Void. The bootstrap must leave
# BASH in the builder (para runs the image-build hook with bash); here it also
# refreshes Void's bundled xbps, a rolling-release snapshot that can be too stale
# to install against. Set it to "" if your base needs nothing.
: "${PARA_IMAGE_BASE:=images:voidlinux}"
: "${PARA_IMAGE_BOOTSTRAP:=xbps-install -Syu xbps bash}"

# Git URL para clones into each workspace. Change it to your project's repo.
# A Parafile sitting at its own repo's toplevel can derive it instead.
# $PARA_PROJECT_DIR is the project root, the directory holding this
# .paraspace/, and para sets it before sourcing this file:
#   : "${PARA_ORIGIN:=$(git -C "$PARA_PROJECT_DIR" remote get-url origin)}"
: "${PARA_ORIGIN:=git@github.com:jchook/docker-caddy.git}"

# A hostname the guest must resolve before para runs any hook in it. Guest DNS
# comes up a beat after the container does, and the provision hook clones over
# the network, so without this the first clone can race it.
: "${PARA_READY_HOST:=github.com}"

# HTTP routes para publishes through its Caddy, one TLS site per "[sub:]port".
# A bare port is the workspace apex; sub:port adds a subdomain (left is where
# you arrive, right is where it goes, as in `docker -p`):
#   8080            -> https://<name>.$PARA_DOMAIN
#   3000,api:3001   -> apex :3000 plus https://api.<name>.$PARA_DOMAIN -> :3001
# Separate entries with commas, spaces, tabs or newlines (para normalizes them),
# so a project with several routes can lay them out to be read:
#   PARA_ROUTES="
#     3000
#     api:3001
#     db:8081
#   "
# Required, because para pins no default port. For a workspace with no HTTP at all
# (a worker, a queue consumer), declare it empty: PARA_ROUTES=""
PARA_ROUTES="${PARA_ROUTES-8080}"

# ---- optional -----------------------------------------------------------------
# para's own defaults, spelled out so you don't have to go look them up. Uncomment
# only what you actually want to change.

# Image `para up` launches, published by `para image build`. Defaults to
# $PARA_PROJECT. Set it only to point several projects at one shared image.
# : "${PARA_IMAGE:=$PARA_PROJECT}"

# Wildcard domain the workspaces live under: https://<name>.$PARA_DOMAIN. Its
# wildcard must resolve to 127.0.0.1, and the default, paraspace.dev, already does.
# : "${PARA_DOMAIN:=paraspace.dev}"

# Directory under ~ to clone into. Default: app
# : "${PARA_CLONE_DIR:=docker-caddy}"

# Shared-volume name, one authenticated home per project by default. Pin several
# projects to a single name to share dotfiles and auth across them.
# : "${PARA_VOLUME:=para-home-$PARA_PROJECT}"

# If your repository is private, enable this: gh authorizes this machine's para
# key for you. Otherwise para prints the key and waits for you to add it by hand.
# : "${PARA_GH_AUTH:=1}"

# para copies the project's own .env into each clone if it has one. Point this at
# a different base .env to seed instead, or set it empty to seed nothing.
# : "${PARA_HOST_ENV:=$PARA_PROJECT_DIR/.env}"

# Docker images to bake into the base image, so `para up` doesn't pull them in
# every workspace. This is THIS PROJECT'S key, not one para knows about: para
# forwards every PARA_* to .paraspace/hooks/image-build, which pre-pulls whatever
# it finds here. Space-separated tags, matching your compose file.
# : "${PARA_PREPULL_IMAGES:=caddy:2-alpine postgres:17-alpine}"
